## Releasing

To release, add a `## [X.Y.Z]` section to `CHANGELOG.md`, then push the tag `vX.Y.Z`. `release.yml` makes the GitHub release from that section.
{% if publish_to_pypi or publish_to_npm or publish_to_ghcr %}
Before the first release, set up the publishing environment
({% for name, on in [('pypi', publish_to_pypi), ('npm', publish_to_npm), ('ghcr', publish_to_ghcr)] if on %}`{{ name }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %})
in Settings > Environments: limit it to tags matching `v*`{% if publish_to_ghcr %} (`ghcr` also to `main`,
where the image is pushed too){% endif %}, and add a required reviewer.
The release workflows explain the rest in their header comments.
{% endif %}{% if 'python' in stacks and kind == 'data-pipeline' %}{% set d = '' if python_dir == '.' else python_dir ~ '/' %}
## Pipeline

[`{{ d }}sources.toml`]({{ d }}sources.toml) declares every input: its URL, its reuse
terms, and whether the pipeline reads it yet (`status`). `just fetch` downloads each
implemented source to `{{ d }}data/raw/` with a fetch record. `just build` writes
`{{ d }}data/processed/`. `just validate` checks the published output against the fetch
records. Neither directory goes into git. [ATTRIBUTION.md](ATTRIBUTION.md) lists what
this repository reuses, and on what terms.

Commit `{{ d }}data/fetches.jsonl` after a fetch that changes it: it holds checksums, not
data, and is the history of every version of each source. Once a week, CI fetches again
and comments on a "Source data revised upstream" issue when a source changed, so keep
Issues turned on for this repository.
{% endif %}{% if 'ts' in stacks and ts_kind == 'web-app' %}{% set d = '' if ts_dir == '.' else ts_dir ~ '/' %}
## Deploy

`pnpm{% if ts_dir != '.' %} -C {{ ts_dir }}{% endif %} dev` serves the app with live reload. Cloudflare Pages deploys it through its Git
integration: create a Pages project from this repository, with build command
`pnpm build`{% if ts_dir != '.' %}, root directory `{{ ts_dir }}`{% endif %} and output directory `dist`. Every push to `main` deploys, and
every pull request gets a preview URL. [`{{ d }}public/_headers`]({{ d }}public/_headers) sets the
response headers, including the Content-Security-Policy that the e2e tests run under.

